1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,018
Hi everyone! Welcome to the IBM Bob Hackathon

2
00:00:01,098 --> 00:00:02,528
I'm Mohammad Jamil Ahmed, and I'm incredibly excited to be here.

3
00:00:02,608 --> 00:00:03,489
I want to be honest with you.

4
00:00:03,569 --> 00:00:04,999
During this build, IBM Bob hit its 40,000-token context limit —

5
00:00:05,079 --> 00:00:05,960
multiple times. The session would fill up,

6
00:00:06,040 --> 00:00:06,920
Bob would lose track of earlier decisions,

7
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:08,430
and I had to export the task, start a fresh session,

8
00:00:08,510 --> 00:00:09,666
and re-anchor it with context from the previous one.

9
00:00:09,746 --> 00:00:10,764
But here's what's remarkable: even with that constraint,

10
00:00:10,844 --> 00:00:12,137
I managed the entire project across five Bob sessions —

11
00:00:12,217 --> 00:00:13,509
exporting tasks, re-loading context, and keeping the build moving forward.

12
00:00:13,589 --> 00:00:15,705
That discipline of working with an AI agent across its limits is itself a real-world skill.

13
00:00:15,785 --> 00:00:17,490
And the result? Nine hundred and eighty-one Bob interactions, five exported session files,

14
00:00:17,570 --> 00:00:18,176
and a fully working product.

15
00:00:18,256 --> 00:00:19,412
Let me share the pain which I have resolved.

16
00:00:19,492 --> 00:00:20,937
Picture a Friday afternoon production incident.

17
00:00:21,017 --> 00:00:22,462
Something broke in the last deployment.

18
00:00:22,542 --> 00:00:25,005
The on-call engineer needs the approved runbook, the change-approval record,

19
00:00:25,085 --> 00:00:26,784
and the audit log — right now.

20
00:00:26,864 --> 00:00:29,327
What actually happens? The official document is in FileNet —

21
00:00:29,407 --> 00:00:31,361
but nobody knows which version was signed off.

22
00:00:31,441 --> 00:00:34,157
The working copy is on a shared drive, in three folders,

23
00:00:34,237 --> 00:00:35,174
with three different names.

24
00:00:35,254 --> 00:00:37,971
The approval lives in an email thread from six months ago.

25
00:00:38,051 --> 00:00:40,513
And the mainframe DMS has never been connected to anything.

26
00:00:40,593 --> 00:00:43,056
That's five systems that don't talk to each other —

27
00:00:43,136 --> 00:00:44,327
and the clock is ticking.

28
00:00:44,407 --> 00:00:45,852
This is not an edge case.

29
00:00:45,932 --> 00:00:48,649
This is every bank, insurer, and government agency running enterprise workloads.

30
00:00:48,729 --> 00:00:51,452
I built C-ECM — Centralized Enterprise Content Management.

31
00:00:51,532 --> 00:00:58,662
It's a governance and intelligence layer that sits in front of every storage system your teams already use

32
00:00:58,742 --> 00:01:03,067
IBM FileNet, IBM i, IBM Z mainframe, SharePoint, Google Drive, S3,

33
00:01:03,147 --> 00:01:06,271
Azure Blob, Box — eleven backends in total.

34
00:01:06,351 --> 00:01:09,475
It looks and feels like a shared drive.

35
00:01:09,555 --> 00:01:13,881
But behind the scenes, every document is now approval-gated, version-controlled, audit-logged,

36
00:01:13,961 --> 00:01:17,886
and cross-system-searchable — on day one, with zero migration required.

37
00:01:17,966 --> 00:01:22,033
One search bar queries all eleven backends in parallel —

38
00:01:22,113 --> 00:01:29,083
not sequentially, so you get results in the time it takes to hit the slowest single system.

39
00:01:29,163 --> 00:01:31,157
Multi-step, quorum-based approval workflows —

40
00:01:31,237 --> 00:01:34,889
the same gate a CAB or change-management process needs.

41
00:01:34,969 --> 00:01:38,622
Every vote, every rejection, every sign-off is permanently recorded.

42
00:01:38,702 --> 00:01:44,428
The compliance dashboard lets you reconstruct any change-approval trail in under five minutes —

43
00:01:44,508 --> 00:01:47,746
not five days. One-click CSV export for auditors.

44
00:01:47,826 --> 00:01:52,307
And IBM watsonx.ai is integrated for document intelligence — summarize, classify,

45
00:01:52,387 --> 00:01:56,869
and ask natural-language questions about any document without even opening it.

46
00:01:56,949 --> 00:02:06,084
This entire project was built end-to-end using IBM Bob in Agent mode —

47
00:02:06,164 --> 00:02:13,880
not as an autocomplete tool, but as a true agentic developer.

48
00:02:13,960 --> 00:02:23,803
Every change was verified against a live 217-test backend suite before being reported complete.

49
00:02:23,883 --> 00:02:35,852
Nine hundred and eighty-one total IBM Bob interactions are exported and committed to the repo as evidence.

50
00:02:35,932 --> 00:02:41,050
C-ECM removes the document-governance tax that every enterprise pays every day —

51
00:02:41,130 --> 00:02:44,515
without asking teams to migrate a single file.

52
00:02:44,595 --> 00:02:47,114
One governance layer. Eleven storage systems.

53
00:02:47,194 --> 00:02:50,146
Zero migration. Built with IBM Bob 2.0.

54
00:02:50,226 --> 00:02:55,424
Thank you. Feel free to reach out to me for any questions.
